because NTV does not exist yet. In 1999, 918 weights (47.4\%) are
strictly positive, while 1,020 (52.6\%) are strictly negative. The
negative weights sum to -2.26. \emph{\uline{σ}fe} = 0\emph{.}91:
\emph{βfe} and \emph{δTR}may be of opposite signs if the standard
deviation of the effect of NTV across subregions is above 0.91
percentage point. \emph{\uline{σ}fe} = 1\emph{.}23: \emph{βfe} may be of
a different sign than the treatment effect in every subregion if the
standard deviation of the effect of NTV across subregions is above 1.23
percentage point, a plausible amount of treatment effect heterogeneity.

Therefore, \emph{βfe} can only receive a causal interpretation if the
effect of NTV is constant across subregions, or if the weights attached
to it are uncorrelated with the intensity of that effect in each
subregion (Assumption 7). These assumptions are not warranted. First, we
estimate\emph{βfe} again, weighting the regression by subregions'
populations. We obtain\emph{βfe} = 14\emph{.}89, more than twice its
value in the unweighted regression, and the difference between the
coefficients is significant (t-stat=2.46). Therefore, we can reject the
null that the treatment effect is constant: if the treatment effect was
constant across subregions, the weighting would not matter so both the
unweighted and the weighted regressions would estimate the same
parameter. Second, the weights attached to\emph{βfe} are correlated with
variables that are likely to be themselves associated with the intensity
of the effect in each subregion. For instance, the correlation between
the weights and subregions' populations is equal to 0.35 (t-stat=14.01).
The effect of NTV may be higher in less populated subregions, as those
regions are more rural and fewer other sources of information may be
available there. This would lead to a violation of Assumption 7.

\textbf{3} \textbf{Extensions of the decomposition results}

We consider hereafter several extensions of our decompositions of
\emph{βfe} and \emph{βfd} in the paper. First, we consider
decompositions under the common trends assumption, and under the
assumption that the treatment effect is stable over time. Second, we
extend our decompositions to ordered treatments. Third, we investigate
the effect of including covariates in the regression. Fourth, we study
two-way fixed effects 2SLS regressions. Throughout, we focus on sharp
designs to ease the exposition. Nevertheless, all the results generalize
to fuzzy designs.

\textbf{3.1} \emph{βfe} \textbf{and} \emph{βfd} \textbf{as weighted sums
of ATEs of switching groups}

We first show that under an additional condition, \emph{βfe} and
\emph{βfd} can be written as a weighted sum of the ATEs of switching
groups.

\textbf{Assumption S2} \emph{(Stable treatment effect) For all g and t
≥}2\emph{,}

\emph{E} (∆\emph{g,t\textbar{}\textbf{D}}) \emph{Dg,t−}1 = \emph{E}
(∆\emph{g,t−}1\emph{\textbar{}\textbf{D}}) \emph{Dg,t−}1\emph{.}

