Theorem S2 shows that in sharp designs, under the common trends and
stable treatment effect

\begin{quote}
assumptions, \emph{βfe} and \emph{βfd} identify weighted sums of ATEs of
switching cells. The weights differ from those in Theorems 1 and 2. Now
the weights attached to \emph{βfe} are all positive in staggered
adoption designs, while the weights attached to \emph{βfd} are all
positive in all sharp designs. Therefore, in staggered adoption (resp.
sharp) designs, \emph{βfe} (resp. \emph{βfd}) relies on the assumption
that the treatment effect is stable over time, but it does not require
that treatment effects be homogeneous

between groups.

