\textbf{3.3} \textbf{Including covariates}

Often times, researchers also include a vector of covariates \emph{Xg,t}
as control variables in their regression. In this section, we show that
our Theorem 1 can be extended to this case.6We start by redefining
Regression 1 in this context.

6Theorem 2 can also be extended to regressions with covariates.
\end{quote}

7

\textbf{Regression 1X} \emph{(Fixed-effects regression with covariates)}

\emph{Let} \emph{βX fedenote the coefficient of Dg,t in an OLS
regression of Yi,g,t on group and period fixed effects, Dg,t, and Xg,t.
Let βX fe}= \emph{EβX fe} \emph{.}

Then, we need to modify Assumptions 3-5. Hereafter, we let
\emph{\textbf{X}g} = (\emph{Xg,}1\emph{, ..., Xg,t}).

\textbf{Assumption S3} \emph{(Independent groups with covariates) The
vectors} (\emph{Yg,t}(0)\emph{, Yg,t}(1)\emph{, Dg,t, Xg,t})1\emph{≤t≤T
are mutually independent.}

\textbf{Assumption S4} \emph{(Strong exogeneity and common trends with
covariates) There is a vector γof same dimension as Xg,t such that}

\begin{quote}
\emph{E} (\emph{Yg,t}(0) \emph{−Yg,t−}1(0) \emph{−}(\emph{Xg,t
−Xg,t−}1)\emph{′γ\textbar{}\textbf{D}g, \textbf{X}g}) = \emph{E}
(\emph{Yg,t}(0) \emph{−Yg,t−}1(0) \emph{−}(\emph{Xg,t
−Xg,t−}1)\emph{′γ})
\end{quote}

\emph{and E} (\emph{Yg,t}(0) \emph{−Yg,t−}1(0) \emph{−}(\emph{Xg,t
−Xg,t−}1)\emph{′γ}) \emph{does not vary across g.}

Rearranging, Assumption S4 requires that

\begin{quote}
\emph{E} (\emph{Yg,t}(0)\emph{\textbar{}\textbf{D}g, \textbf{X}g})
\emph{−E} (\emph{Yg,t−}1(0)\emph{\textbar{}\textbf{D}g, \textbf{X}g}) =
(\emph{Xg,t −Xg,t−}1)\emph{′γ} + \emph{λt,}
\end{quote}

for some constant \emph{λt}. Then, Assumption S4 allows for the
possibility that groups experience different evolutions of their
\emph{Yg,t}(0) over time, but it requires that those differential
evolutions are fully accounted for by a linear model in \emph{Xg,t
−Xg,t−}1, the change in a group's covariates. Assumption S4 is implied
by the linear model that is often invoked to justify the use of the FE
regression with covariates. For instance, the use of Regression 1X is
often justified by the following model:

