Equation (24) implies Assumption S4, but it does not imply Assumption 5.

An interesting special case is when the control variables are
group-specific linear trends. Then, Assumption s4 requires that for all
\emph{t ≥}2,

\begin{quote}
\emph{E} (\emph{Yg,t}(0)\emph{\textbar{}\textbf{D}g, \textbf{X}g})
\emph{−E} (\emph{Yg,t−}1(0)\emph{\textbar{}\textbf{D}g, \textbf{X}g}) =
\emph{γg} + \emph{λt,}
\end{quote}

for some constants \emph{γg} and \emph{λt}. From \emph{t −}1 to
\emph{t}, the evolution of \emph{Y} (0) in group \emph{g} should deviate
from its group-specific linear trend \emph{γg} by an amount \emph{λt}
common to all groups. Then, Assumption S4 is a ``common deviation from
linear trends'' assumption, which may be more plausible than the
standard common trends assumption.

