We now establish the asymptotic properties of DIDM and
CI1\emph{−α}(\emph{δS}) under the following assump-tions. Hereafter, we
denote \emph{\textbf{U}} = (\emph{P}0\emph{,}0\emph{,}1\emph{,
Q}0\emph{,}0\emph{,}1\emph{, ..., P}1\emph{,}1\emph{,T,
Q}1\emph{,}1\emph{,T}).

\textbf{Assumption S9} \emph{(Existence of moments and limits)}
sup\emph{g,t Ng,t \textless{}} +\emph{∞and} sup(\emph{d,g,t})
\emph{E}(\emph{Y}4 \emph{g,t}(\emph{d})) \emph{\textless{}} +\emph{∞.}
lim\emph{G E}{[}\emph{\textbf{U}}{]} \emph{and} lim\emph{G G × V}
(\emph{\textbf{U}}) \emph{exist.}

\textbf{Assumption S10} \emph{(Positive probability of ``stable'' groups
and existence of switchers) For all} (\emph{d, g, t})
\emph{∈\{}0\emph{,}
1\emph{\}×}(N\emph{\textbackslash\{}0\emph{\}})\emph{×\{}2\emph{, ...,
T\},} Pr(\emph{Dg,t} = 1\emph{−d, Dg,t−}1 = \emph{d})
\emph{\textgreater{}} 0 \emph{implies} lim\emph{G
E}{[}\emph{P}1\emph{−d,d,t}{]} \emph{\textgreater{}} 0 \emph{and}
lim\emph{G E}{[}\emph{Pd,d,t}{]} \emph{\textgreater{}} 0\emph{.
Moreover,} lim\emph{G E}{[}\emph{P}0\emph{,}1\emph{,t} +
\emph{P}1\emph{,}0\emph{,t}{]} \emph{\textgreater{}} 0 \emph{for at
least one t.}

Assumption S9 imposes the (uniform) existence of moments of order 4 of
\emph{Yg,t}(\emph{d}), and that some non-random averages converge as
\emph{G} tends to infinity. These assumptions ensure that we can apply
law of large numbers and central limit theorems in our set-up where
groups are independent but not necessarily identically distributed.
Assumption S10 imposes that when at least one group switches from
\emph{d} to 1 \emph{−d} with a positive probability on a given period,
then on average over all groups, the limit probabilities of switching
from \emph{d} to 1 \emph{−d} will be positive as \emph{G →∞}. The limit
probability of remaining at \emph{d} will also be positive. This latter
condition may be seen as a weaker version of Assumption 11, as it
imposes the existence of ``stable'' groups only

12

with probability tending to one as \emph{G →∞}. The last condition in
Assumption S10 simply states that asymptotically, the proportion of
switchers is strictly postive.

The following result shows that under these conditions, DIDM is
asymptotically normal, and that CI1\emph{−α}(\emph{δS}) is
asymptotically conservative.

