\emph{Proof that wS fd,g,t≥}0\\
We just have to focus on the cases where \emph{Dg,t ̸}= \emph{Dg,t−}1.
Note that \emph{εfd,g,t} = \emph{Dg,t −Dg,t−}1 \emph{−}(\emph{D.,t
−D.,t−}1). Then, if \emph{Dg,t −Dg,t−}1 = 1, the numerator of \emph{wS
fd,g,t}has the same sign as 1\emph{−}(\emph{D.,t −D.,t−}1), which is
positive. If \emph{Dg,t −Dg,t−}1 = \emph{−}1, the numerator of \emph{wS
fd,g,t}has the same sign as 1 + (\emph{D.,t −D.,t−}1), which is also
positive. Because the denominator sums terms that are always positive,
it is positive as well. The result follows.

